### (D)TLS Protected SCTP
* Presentation: [(D)TLS Protected SCTP](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-tsvwg-dtls-protected-sctp-00)
* Magnus Westerlund presented updates on [draft-ietf-tsvwg-sctp-dtls-chunk](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-tsvwg-sctp-dtls-chunk/). Version -07 adds an API notification when receiving packets under a new key context, moves PPID registrations to key management specifications, and incorporates editorial feedback from Russ Housley. The draft is ready for WGLC.
* Key management transition: Magnus Westerlund presented `draft-westerlund-tsvwg-dtls-handshake-03` as a simplified alternative to [draft-ietf-tsvwg-dtls-chunk-key-management-01](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-tsvwg-dtls-chunk-key-management-01/). The individual draft relies on standard TLS 1.3 exporters without requiring Extended Key Update extensions or RFC 9261 exported authenticators.
* Jan Lindblad and Michael Tuexen supported switching to the simpler individual draft, noting easier deployment across existing TLS 1.3 stacks without technical or security drawbacks.
* To gauge working group sentiment, the chairs ran three polls:
  * **Poll 1**: *I have familiarized myself with dtls-handshake-03* — yes: 9, no: 31, no_opinion: 6 (total: 73)
  * **Poll 2**: *I would support adoption of dtls-handshake-03* — yes: 13, no: 1, no_opinion: 33 (total: 73)
  * **Poll 3**: *Provided that dtls-hanshake-03 is adopted, TSVWG should "unadopt " chunk-key-management-01* — yes: 16, no: 2, no_opinion: 17 (total: 72)
* The chairs determined that a design team meeting will be scheduled within 2–3 weeks to discuss objections raised on the mailing list before initiating a formal adoption call.

### SRM for L4S
* Presentation: [SRM for L4S](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-tsvwg-srm-for-l4s-00)
* Miroslav Kovac presented Structured Rate Management (SRM) for L4S (`draft-kovac-tsvwg-srm`), designed for high-capacity edge routers (e.g., BNGs) with deep buffers. SRM uses token bucket policers to mark CE bits when L4S traffic exceeds Committed Information Rates (CIR), avoiding the need for per-flow buffer delay measurements or complex DualQ AQM hardware implementations.
* Stuart Cheshire highlighted that policing via token buckets models a virtual buffer to signal L4S senders without introducing physical packet delay, and offered to assist in refining the text.
* Christian Huitema, Greg White, and Chris Box spoke in favor of the work, noting it simplifies edge router queuing and provides necessary operational guidance referenced in [draft-ietf-tsvwg-l4sops](https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-ietf-tsvwg-l4sops/).
* Gorry Fairhurst, Mirja Kühlewind, and Ana N requested additional comparative performance data under cross-traffic and variable link capacity before proceeding with adoption.

### L4S in Cellular Networks
* Presentation: [L4S in Cellular Networks](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-tsvwg-l4s-in-cellular-networks-00)
* Stuart Cheshire shared empirical speed test latency measurements, demonstrating that L4S on cable networks maintains 17 ms uplink latency under 100% saturation, whereas current cellular connections experience over 1 second of downlink queuing delay and 0.5 seconds of uplink queuing delay.
* Vidhi Goel detailed cellular L4S operation in 5G. While downlink AQM in the gNodeB operates effectively, uplink queuing occurs on the user equipment (UE). Under current 3GPP specifications, the gNodeB infers UE buffer buildup via coarse Buffer Status Reports (BSR) and performs CE marking on behalf of the UE.
* Vidhi Goel raised the question of whether uplink queuing should be managed directly by UEs using separate L4S QoS bearers/flows. Chris Box and Koen De Schepper noted that because cellular link capacity fluctuates rapidly, the gNodeB scheduler remains best positioned to perform marking based on dynamic grant allocations.

### 443 is Enough
* Presentation: [443 is enough](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-tsvwg-443-is-enough-00)
* Brian Trammell presented `draft-trammell-tsvwg-443-is-enough`, proposing an update to RFC 7605 port registration guidance. The document addresses automated AI coding agents and developers requesting dedicated transport ports for web-adjacent, HTTP REST APIs instead of multiplexing over port 443 using ALPN, SNI, and path identifiers.
* Magnus Westerlund, Lorenzo Colitti, Christian Huitema, Lars Eggert, and Mike Bishop discussed refining the draft to explicitly detail network ossification and aggressive NAT timeout penalties associated with non-443 ports, which provides clear incentives for automated agents to select port 443.
* Strong support for adoption was expressed. The author will incorporate feedback prior to a formal adoption call.

### TCP and UDP Checksum Calculations for ILNP
* Presentation: [TCP and UDP checksum calculations for ILNP](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-tsvwg-tcp-and-udp-checksum-calculations-for-ilnp-01)
* Saleem Bhatti presented `draft-bhatti-tsvwg-ilnp-checksum`, proposing to update Identifier-Locator Network Protocol (ILNP) checksum calculations to cover the full 128-bit IPv6 address field, aligning ILNP wire formats with standard IPv6 stacks.
* Lars Eggert, Zaheduzzaman Sarker, and Lorenzo Colitti noted that because the underlying ILNP specifications (RFCs 6740–6748) originated from the IRTF and are experimental, the proposed update should proceed via the Independent Submission Stream (ISE) or as an individual document rather than a TSVWG standards-track RFC.

### SNAP Updates
* Presentation: [SNAP updates](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-tsvwg-snap-updates-00)
* Philipp Tiesel presented `draft-tiesel-tsvwg-sdp-sctp-snap`, specifying the SCTP Negotiation Acceleration Protocol (SNAP) to accelerate WebRTC data channel establishment by embedding SCTP INIT chunks within SDP signaling.
* An ongoing Chrome origin trial demonstrated a reduction of up to 6 round-trip times (RTTs) at the 95th percentile during connection handshakes.
* The working group discussed SDP syntax choices: a Base64-encoded SCTP INIT blob versus key-value TLV attributes in SDP. Niels ten Oever and Jonathan Lennox favored the Base64 approach to minimize redundant parsing.

